@lang en
IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `ca-interpretation-nouns`

-- ca-interpretation-reading.l4
--
-- The Interpretation Act (Canada), R.S.C., 1985, c. I-21: the rules for reading an enactment that pass 1 left out.  Pass 2.
-- Sections 3(2), 3(3), 4, 5(1), 8(3), 8.1, 8.3, 10, 12, 13, 14, 15, 16, 18(1) and (2), 21(2), 25(2), 33, 36, 38, 40, 41(2) to (5), 42 and 45.
-- Imports the nouns module and the standard library.  The definitions module imports this one (for section 3(2)).
--
-- THE HOUSE RULE FOR A RULE THAT HAS A STATED CONDITION.  The answer is TRUE where the text states the condition and it is met.
-- It is FALSE only where the text says "shall not be deemed", "does not include" or "means" (which gives the whole meaning).
-- Where the case asked about is not one the text addresses, the answer is a refusal, never FALSE.
-- Each refusal is a named function at a generic type, so that its name says what the text leaves open.

§ `Section 3(2): the Act applies to its own interpretation`

-- Plain gloss: the provisions of the Act apply to the interpretation of the Act.
-- FORK F-3-2: section 3(1) says "unless a contrary intention appears" and section 3(2) does not.  Taken: section 3(2) is flat, because
-- it is a separate subsection with no qualifier and would add nothing if it only repeated section 3(1) for the one enactment that is the
-- Act.  The other reading carries the qualifier of section 3(1) into section 3(2).
-- src:31 | (2) The provisions of this Act apply to the interpretation of this Act.
@ref Interpretation Act s 3(2) (src:31)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a provision of the Act applies to the interpretation of the Act` MEANS
    TRUE

§ `Section 3(3): a rule of construction that the Act does not exclude`

-- Plain gloss: nothing in the Act stops a rule of construction from applying to an enactment if the rule applies to that enactment and is
-- not inconsistent with the Act.  The text says nothing of a rule that does not apply, or that is inconsistent.
-- src:33 | (3) Nothing in this Act excludes the application to an enactment of a rule of construction applicable to that enactment and not inconsistent with this Act.
@ref Interpretation Act s 3(3) (src:33)
GIVEN `the rule is applicable to that enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the rule is not inconsistent with the Act` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the Act does not exclude the application to an enactment of a rule of construction which` `the rule is applicable to that enactment` `and` `the rule is not inconsistent with the Act` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the rule is applicable to that enactment` AND `the rule is not inconsistent with the Act` THEN TRUE
           OTHERWISE `section 3(3) protects a rule that is applicable and not inconsistent and does not say what becomes of any other`

-- src:33 | (3) Nothing in this Act excludes the application to an enactment of a rule of construction applicable to that enactment and not inconsistent with this Act.
@ref Interpretation Act s 3(3) (src:33)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 3(3) protects a rule that is applicable and not inconsistent and does not say what becomes of any other` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 3(3) protects a rule that is applicable and not inconsistent and does not say what becomes of any other"

§ `Section 4: the enacting clause of an Act`

-- Plain gloss: the enacting clause of an Act may be in the quoted form.  It follows the preamble, if there is one, and the provisions of the
-- body follow it in a concise and enunciative form.
-- src:37 | 4 (1) The enacting clause of an Act may be in the following form:
-- src:38 | “Her Majesty, by and with the advice and consent of the Senate and House of Commons of Canada, enacts as follows:”.
@ref Interpretation Act s 4(1) (src:37-38)
GIVEN `the enacting clause as written` IS A STRING
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the enacting clause of an Act may be in the form` `the enacting clause as written` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the enacting clause as written` EQUALS "Her Majesty, by and with the advice and consent of the Senate and House of Commons of Canada, enacts as follows:" THEN TRUE
           OTHERWISE `section 4(1) says the enacting clause may be in the form given and does not say that it may not be in another`

-- src:37 | 4 (1) The enacting clause of an Act may be in the following form:
-- src:38 | “Her Majesty, by and with the advice and consent of the Senate and House of Commons of Canada, enacts as follows:”.
@ref Interpretation Act s 4(1) (src:37-38)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 4(1) says the enacting clause may be in the form given and does not say that it may not be in another` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 4(1) says the enacting clause may be in the form given and does not say that it may not be in another"

-- "concise and enunciative form" is a standard a person judges; the caller states the judgment.
-- src:40 | (2) The enacting clause of an Act shall follow the preamble, if any, and the various provisions within the purview or body of the Act shall follow in a concise and enunciative form.
@ref Interpretation Act s 4(2) (src:40)
GIVEN `the Act has a preamble` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the enacting clause follows the preamble` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provisions of the body follow the enacting clause` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the provisions are in a concise and enunciative form` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an Act is arranged as section 4(2) requires where` `the Act has a preamble` `and the enacting clause follows it` `the enacting clause follows the preamble` `and the provisions follow the clause` `the provisions of the body follow the enacting clause` `and are concise and enunciative` `the provisions are in a concise and enunciative form` MEANS
        ((NOT `the Act has a preamble`) OR `the enacting clause follows the preamble`)
    AND `the provisions of the body follow the enacting clause`
    AND `the provisions are in a concise and enunciative form`

§ `Section 5(1): the endorsement of the day of assent`

-- Plain gloss: the Clerk of the Parliaments must endorse on every Act, immediately after its title, the day, month and year of assent in
-- Her Majesty's name, and the endorsement is part of the Act.  The text names no one who may demand the endorsement.
DECLARE `An endorsement of the day of assent` HAS
    `where it is placed`        IS A STRING
    `the day it records`        IS A DATE
    `it is a part of the Act`   IS A BOOLEAN

-- src:46 | 5 (1) The Clerk of the Parliaments shall endorse on every Act, immediately after its title, the day, month and year when the Act was assented to in Her Majesty’s name and the endorsement shall be a part of the Act.
@ref Interpretation Act s 5(1) (src:46)
GIVEN `the day the Act was assented to` IS A DATE
GIVETH AN `An endorsement of the day of assent`
`the endorsement the Clerk of the Parliaments shall make on an Act assented to on` `the day the Act was assented to` MEANS
    `An endorsement of the day of assent`
        WITH `where it is placed` IS "immediately after the title of the Act"
             `the day it records` IS `the day the Act was assented to`
             `it is a part of the Act` IS TRUE

§ `Section 8(3): an old Act with extra-territorial operation`

-- Plain gloss: an Act now in force that was enacted before December 11, 1931 and was meant, expressly or by necessary or reasonable
-- implication, to have extra-territorial operation, even in part, is construed as if Parliament had had full power to make laws having
-- extra-territorial operation when it was enacted.  The text says nothing of any other Act.
-- src:81 | (3) Every Act now in force enacted prior to December 11, 1931 that expressly or by necessary or reasonable implication was intended, as to the whole or any part thereof, to have extra-territorial operation shall be construed as if, at the date of its enactment, the Parliament of Canada had full power to make laws having extra-territorial operation as provided by the Statute of Westminster, 1931.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8(3) (src:81)
GIVEN `it is now in force` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the day it was enacted` IS A DATE
      `it was intended, expressly or by necessary or reasonable implication, to have extra-territorial operation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an Act is construed as if Parliament had full power to make laws having extra-territorial operation where` `it is now in force` `and was enacted on` `the day it was enacted` `and` `it was intended, expressly or by necessary or reasonable implication, to have extra-territorial operation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `it is now in force` AND `the day it was enacted` < YMD 1931 12 11 AND `it was intended, expressly or by necessary or reasonable implication, to have extra-territorial operation`
        THEN TRUE
        OTHERWISE `section 8(3) speaks of an Act now in force, enacted before December 11, 1931, meant to have extra-territorial operation, and says nothing of any other`

-- src:81 | (3) Every Act now in force enacted prior to December 11, 1931 that expressly or by necessary or reasonable implication was intended, as to the whole or any part thereof, to have extra-territorial operation shall be construed as if, at the date of its enactment, the Parliament of Canada had full power to make laws having extra-territorial operation as provided by the Statute of Westminster, 1931.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8(3) (src:81)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 8(3) speaks of an Act now in force, enacted before December 11, 1931, meant to have extra-territorial operation, and says nothing of any other` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 8(3) speaks of an Act now in force, enacted before December 11, 1931, meant to have extra-territorial operation, and says nothing of any other"

§ `Section 8.1: the common law and the civil law`

-- Plain gloss: the common law and the civil law are equally authoritative and recognized sources of the law of property and civil rights in
-- Canada.  Unless otherwise provided by law, when interpreting an enactment requires reference to a province's rules, principles or concepts of
-- that law, reference is made to the ones in force in the province at the time the enactment is being applied.
DECLARE `A source of the law of property and civil rights` IS ONE OF
    `the common law`
    `the civil law`
    `a source of neither kind`

DECLARE `Which time's rules are referred to` IS ONE OF
    `those in force in the province at the time the enactment is being applied`
    `those in force at some other time`

-- src:87 | 8.1 Both the common law and the civil law are equally authoritative and recognized sources of the law of property and civil rights in Canada and, unless otherwise provided by law, if in interpreting an enactment it is necessary to refer to a province’s rules, principles or concepts forming part of the law of property and civil rights, reference must be made to the rules, principles and concepts in force in the province at the time the enactment is being applied.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8.1 (src:87)
GIVEN s IS A `A source of the law of property and civil rights`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`is an equally authoritative and recognized source of the law of property and civil rights in Canada` s MEANS
    CONSIDER s
    WHEN `the common law`            THEN TRUE
    WHEN `the civil law`             THEN TRUE
    WHEN `a source of neither kind`  THEN `section 8.1 names the common law and the civil law and does not say whether any other source is equally authoritative`

-- src:87 | 8.1 Both the common law and the civil law are equally authoritative and recognized sources of the law of property and civil rights in Canada and, unless otherwise provided by law, if in interpreting an enactment it is necessary to refer to a province’s rules, principles or concepts forming part of the law of property and civil rights, reference must be made to the rules, principles and concepts in force in the province at the time the enactment is being applied.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8.1 (src:87)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 8.1 names the common law and the civil law and does not say whether any other source is equally authoritative` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 8.1 names the common law and the civil law and does not say whether any other source is equally authoritative"

-- src:87 | 8.1 Both the common law and the civil law are equally authoritative and recognized sources of the law of property and civil rights in Canada and, unless otherwise provided by law, if in interpreting an enactment it is necessary to refer to a province’s rules, principles or concepts forming part of the law of property and civil rights, reference must be made to the rules, principles and concepts in force in the province at the time the enactment is being applied.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8.1 (src:87)
GIVEN `it is otherwise provided by law` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Which time's rules are referred to`
`the province's rules, principles and concepts to which reference is made, unless` `it is otherwise provided by law` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `it is otherwise provided by law` THEN `another law decides which time's rules of the province are referred to and section 8.1 does not say which`
           OTHERWISE `those in force in the province at the time the enactment is being applied`

-- src:87 | 8.1 Both the common law and the civil law are equally authoritative and recognized sources of the law of property and civil rights in Canada and, unless otherwise provided by law, if in interpreting an enactment it is necessary to refer to a province’s rules, principles or concepts forming part of the law of property and civil rights, reference must be made to the rules, principles and concepts in force in the province at the time the enactment is being applied.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8.1 (src:87)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`another law decides which time's rules of the province are referred to and section 8.1 does not say which` MEANS
    REFUSE "another law decides which time's rules of the province are referred to and section 8.1 does not say which"

§ `Section 8.3: the rights of Indigenous peoples`

-- CONVENTION C-3-1-GATE: section 3(1) lets "a contrary intention" displace "every provision of this Act", including this one.  The function
-- below has no contrary-intention input, as no rule of the Act except section 8(1) (which says so itself) has one; the contrary intention
-- is asked once, by the gate `a provision of the Act applies to an enactment unless` (rules module), which a caller applies first.
-- Plain gloss: every enactment is construed as upholding the Aboriginal and treaty rights of Indigenous peoples that section 35 of the
-- Constitution Act, 1982 recognizes and affirms, and not as abrogating or derogating from them.  "Indigenous peoples" has the meaning the
-- definition "aboriginal peoples of Canada" gives in subsection 35(2) of the Constitution Act, 1982.
-- src:93 | 8.3 (1) Every enactment is to be construed as upholding the Aboriginal and treaty rights of Indigenous peoples recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982, and not as abrogating or derogating from them.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8.3(1) (src:93)
GIVEN `the right is an Aboriginal or treaty right of Indigenous peoples recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the construction abrogates or derogates from the right` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a construction of an enactment is permitted as to a right where` `the right is an Aboriginal or treaty right of Indigenous peoples recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982` `and` `the construction abrogates or derogates from the right` MEANS
    BRANCH IF NOT `the right is an Aboriginal or treaty right of Indigenous peoples recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982`
               THEN `section 8.3(1) speaks of the Aboriginal and treaty rights recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982 and of no other right`
           IF `the construction abrogates or derogates from the right` THEN FALSE
           OTHERWISE TRUE

-- src:93 | 8.3 (1) Every enactment is to be construed as upholding the Aboriginal and treaty rights of Indigenous peoples recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982, and not as abrogating or derogating from them.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8.3(1) (src:93)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 8.3(1) speaks of the Aboriginal and treaty rights recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982 and of no other right` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 8.3(1) speaks of the Aboriginal and treaty rights recognized and affirmed by section 35 of the Constitution Act, 1982 and of no other right"

-- src:95 | (2) For the purposes of subsection (1), Indigenous peoples has the meaning assigned by the definition aboriginal peoples of Canada in subsection 35(2) of the Constitution Act, 1982.
@ref Interpretation Act s 8.3(2) (src:95)
GIVEN `the people are within the definition aboriginal peoples of Canada in subsection 35(2) of the Constitution Act, 1982` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a people are Indigenous peoples for section 8.3(1) where` `the people are within the definition aboriginal peoples of Canada in subsection 35(2) of the Constitution Act, 1982` MEANS
    `the people are within the definition aboriginal peoples of Canada in subsection 35(2) of the Constitution Act, 1982`

§ `Section 10: the law is always speaking`

-- Plain gloss: the law is considered as always speaking.  A thing expressed in the present tense is applied to the circumstances as they
-- arise, so that effect is given to the enactment according to its true spirit, intent and meaning.
DECLARE `A tense of expression` IS ONE OF
    `the present tense`
    `some other tense`

DECLARE `The circumstances a provision is applied to` IS ONE OF
    `the circumstances as they arise`
    `the circumstances as they stood when it was enacted`

-- src:103 | 10 The law shall be considered as always speaking, and where a matter or thing is expressed in the present tense, it shall be applied to the circumstances as they arise, so that effect may be given to the enactment according to its true spirit, intent and meaning.
@ref Interpretation Act s 10 (src:103)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`the law is considered as always speaking` MEANS
    TRUE

-- src:103 | 10 The law shall be considered as always speaking, and where a matter or thing is expressed in the present tense, it shall be applied to the circumstances as they arise, so that effect may be given to the enactment according to its true spirit, intent and meaning.
@ref Interpretation Act s 10 (src:103)
GIVEN t IS A `A tense of expression`
GIVETH A `The circumstances a provision is applied to`
`the circumstances to which a matter or thing is applied, if it is expressed in` t MEANS
    CONSIDER t
    WHEN `the present tense` THEN `the circumstances as they arise`
    WHEN `some other tense`  THEN `section 10 speaks of the present tense and does not say to which circumstances another tense is applied`

-- src:103 | 10 The law shall be considered as always speaking, and where a matter or thing is expressed in the present tense, it shall be applied to the circumstances as they arise, so that effect may be given to the enactment according to its true spirit, intent and meaning.
@ref Interpretation Act s 10 (src:103)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 10 speaks of the present tense and does not say to which circumstances another tense is applied` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 10 speaks of the present tense and does not say to which circumstances another tense is applied"

§ `Section 12: enactments are remedial`

-- Plain gloss: every enactment is deemed remedial and is given the fair, large and liberal construction and interpretation that best ensures
-- the attainment of its objects.
-- src:111 | 12 Every enactment is deemed remedial, and shall be given such fair, large and liberal construction and interpretation as best ensures the attainment of its objects.
@ref Interpretation Act s 12 (src:111)
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an enactment is deemed remedial` MEANS
    TRUE

-- src:111 | 12 Every enactment is deemed remedial, and shall be given such fair, large and liberal construction and interpretation as best ensures the attainment of its objects.
@ref Interpretation Act s 12 (src:111)
GIVEN `the construction is fair, large and liberal` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the construction best ensures the attainment of the objects of the enactment` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a construction is the one section 12 gives where` `the construction is fair, large and liberal` `and` `the construction best ensures the attainment of the objects of the enactment` MEANS
        `the construction is fair, large and liberal`
    AND `the construction best ensures the attainment of the objects of the enactment`

§ `Section 13: the preamble`

-- Plain gloss: the preamble of an enactment is read as a part of the enactment intended to assist in explaining its purport and object.
DECLARE `The standing of a printed part of an enactment` IS ONE OF
    `a part of the enactment, intended to assist in explaining its purport and object`
    `no part of the enactment, inserted for convenience of reference only`

-- src:115 | 13 The preamble of an enactment shall be read as a part of the enactment intended to assist in explaining its purport and object.
@ref Interpretation Act s 13 (src:115)
GIVEN p IS A `A part of an enactment as printed`
GIVETH A `The standing of a printed part of an enactment`
`the standing section 13 gives to` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN `the preamble`  THEN `a part of the enactment, intended to assist in explaining its purport and object`
    WHEN `a marginal note` THEN `section 13 speaks of the preamble and of nothing else printed`
    WHEN `a reference to a former enactment that appears after the end of a section or other division` THEN `section 13 speaks of the preamble and of nothing else printed`
    WHEN `a reference to a former enactment that appears elsewhere in the printed text` THEN `section 13 speaks of the preamble and of nothing else printed`
    WHEN `another part of the printed text` THEN `section 13 speaks of the preamble and of nothing else printed`

-- src:115 | 13 The preamble of an enactment shall be read as a part of the enactment intended to assist in explaining its purport and object.
@ref Interpretation Act s 13 (src:115)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 13 speaks of the preamble and of nothing else printed` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 13 speaks of the preamble and of nothing else printed"

§ `Section 14: marginal notes and historical references`

-- Plain gloss: marginal notes, and references to former enactments that appear after the end of a section or other division, form no part
-- of the enactment; they are inserted for convenience of reference only.
-- FORK F-14-SCOPE: "that appear after the end of a section or other division" can qualify only "references to former enactments" or both
-- that and "marginal notes".  Taken: it qualifies the references only, because a marginal note is not printed after the end of a section
-- and the sentence would then say nothing of marginal notes.  The other reading leaves marginal notes outside the rule.
-- A reference to a former enactment that appears elsewhere in the printed text is not one the text addresses.
-- src:117 | 14 Marginal notes and references to former enactments that appear after the end of a section or other division in an enactment form no part of the enactment, but are inserted for convenience of reference only.
@ref Interpretation Act s 14 (src:117)
GIVEN p IS A `A part of an enactment as printed`
GIVETH A `The standing of a printed part of an enactment`
`the standing section 14 gives to` p MEANS
    CONSIDER p
    WHEN `a marginal note` THEN `no part of the enactment, inserted for convenience of reference only`
    WHEN `a reference to a former enactment that appears after the end of a section or other division` THEN `no part of the enactment, inserted for convenience of reference only`
    WHEN `the preamble` THEN `section 14 speaks of marginal notes and of references to former enactments after the end of a section or division and of nothing else printed`
    WHEN `a reference to a former enactment that appears elsewhere in the printed text` THEN `section 14 speaks of marginal notes and of references to former enactments after the end of a section or division and of nothing else printed`
    WHEN `another part of the printed text` THEN `section 14 speaks of marginal notes and of references to former enactments after the end of a section or division and of nothing else printed`

-- src:117 | 14 Marginal notes and references to former enactments that appear after the end of a section or other division in an enactment form no part of the enactment, but are inserted for convenience of reference only.
@ref Interpretation Act s 14 (src:117)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 14 speaks of marginal notes and of references to former enactments after the end of a section or division and of nothing else printed` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 14 speaks of marginal notes and of references to former enactments after the end of a section or division and of nothing else printed"

§ `Section 15: definitions and interpretation provisions in an enactment`

-- Plain gloss: definitions or rules of interpretation in an enactment apply to every provision of the enactment, including the provisions
-- that contain them.  An interpretation section or provision is read as applicable only if a contrary intention does not appear, and as
-- applicable to all other enactments on the same subject-matter unless a contrary intention appears.
-- FORK F-15: subsection (1) states the reach without a condition and subsection (2)(a) says "applicable only if a contrary intention does
-- not appear".  Taken: (2)(a) qualifies (1), so a contrary intention displaces a definition even in its own enactment.  The other reading
-- keeps (1) unconditional and gives (2)(a) no work to do.
-- src:121 | 15 (1) Definitions or rules of interpretation in an enactment apply to all the provisions of the enactment, including the provisions that contain those definitions or rules of interpretation.
-- src:124 | (a) as being applicable only if a contrary intention does not appear; and
@ref Interpretation Act s 15(1) (src:121) and s 15(2)(a) (src:124)
GIVEN `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a definition or rule of interpretation in an enactment applies to a provision of the same enactment, including the provision that contains it, unless` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    NOT `a contrary intention appears`

-- src:125 | (b) as being applicable to all other enactments relating to the same subject-matter unless a contrary intention appears.
@ref Interpretation Act s 15(2)(b) (src:125)
GIVEN `the other enactment relates to the same subject-matter` IS A BOOLEAN
      `a contrary intention appears` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`an interpretation provision of an enactment applies to another enactment where` `the other enactment relates to the same subject-matter` `unless` `a contrary intention appears` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the other enactment relates to the same subject-matter` THEN NOT `a contrary intention appears`
           OTHERWISE `section 15(2)(b) speaks of other enactments relating to the same subject-matter and says nothing of any other`

-- src:125 | (b) as being applicable to all other enactments relating to the same subject-matter unless a contrary intention appears.
@ref Interpretation Act s 15(2)(b) (src:125)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 15(2)(b) speaks of other enactments relating to the same subject-matter and says nothing of any other` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 15(2)(b) speaks of other enactments relating to the same subject-matter and says nothing of any other"

§ `Section 16: words in regulations`

-- Plain gloss: where an enactment confers power to make regulations, the expressions used in the regulations have the same respective
-- meanings as in the enactment that confers the power.
-- src:127 | 16 Where an enactment confers power to make regulations, expressions used in the regulations have the same respective meanings as in the enactment conferring the power.
@ref Interpretation Act s 16 (src:127)
GIVEN `the enactment confers power to make the regulations` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the meaning of the expression in the enactment conferring the power` IS A STRING
GIVETH A STRING
`the meaning of an expression used in regulations made under an enactment which` `the enactment confers power to make the regulations` `gives the expression the meaning` `the meaning of the expression in the enactment conferring the power` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the enactment confers power to make the regulations` THEN `the meaning of the expression in the enactment conferring the power`
           OTHERWISE `section 16 speaks of regulations made under a power an enactment confers and says nothing of any other instrument`

-- src:127 | 16 Where an enactment confers power to make regulations, expressions used in the regulations have the same respective meanings as in the enactment conferring the power.
@ref Interpretation Act s 16 (src:127)
GIVEN a IS A TYPE
GIVETH AN a
`section 16 speaks of regulations made under a power an enactment confers and says nothing of any other instrument` MEANS
    REFUSE "section 16 speaks of regulations made under a power an enactment confers and says nothing of any other instrument"

§ `Section 18(1) and (2): proclamations`

-- Plain gloss: where an enactment authorizes the issue of a proclamation, it is understood to be a proclamation of the Governor in
-- Council.  Where the Governor General is authorized to issue one, it is understood to be issued under an order of the Governor in Council,
-- and the proclamation need not say so.
-- src:135 | 18 (1) Where an enactment authorizes the issue of a proclamation, the proclamation shall be understood to be a proclamation of the Governor in Council.
@ref Interpretation Act s 18(1) (src:135)
GIVEN `an enactment authorizes the issue of the proclamation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A STRING
`a proclamation is understood to be a proclamation of whom, where` `an enactment authorizes the issue of the proclamation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `an enactment authorizes the issue of the proclamation` THEN "the Governor in Council"
           OTHERWISE `section 18 speaks of a proclamation an enactment authorizes and says nothing of any other`

-- src:137 | (2) Where the Governor General is authorized to issue a proclamation, the proclamation shall be understood to be a proclamation issued under an order of the Governor in Council, but it is not necessary to mention in the proclamation that it is issued under such an order.
@ref Interpretation Act s 18(2) (src:137)
GIVEN `the Governor General is authorized to issue the proclamation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`a proclamation is understood to be issued under an order of the Governor in Council, where` `the Governor General is authorized to issue the proclamation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the Governor General is authorized to issue the proclamation` THEN TRUE
           OTHERWISE `section 18 speaks of a proclamation an enactment authorizes and says nothing of any other`

-- src:137 | (2) Where the Governor General is authorized to issue a proclamation, the proclamation shall be understood to be a proclamation issued under an order of the Governor in Council, but it is not necessary to mention in the proclamation that it is issued under such an order.
@ref Interpretation Act s 18(2) (src:137)
GIVEN `the Governor General is authorized to issue the proclamation` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`it is necessary to mention in the proclamation that it is issued under an order, where` `the Governor General is authorized to issue the proclamation` MEANS
    BRANCH IF `the Governor General is authorized to issue the proclamation` THEN FALSE
           OTHERWISE `section 18 speaks of a proclamation an enactment authorizes and says nothing of any other`

